                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Sigma ($\\sigma$) Bonds:** Each C\u2013H bond is a single **sigma ($\\sigma$) bond**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 680,
                "end_index": 779,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This type of bond is formed by the \"head-on\" overlap of atomic orbitals along the internuclear axis"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 794,
                "end_index": 916,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Hybridization of Carbon ($\\text{sp}^3$)\nIn its ground state, a carbon atom has the electron configuration $1s^2 2s^2 2p^2$"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 918,
                "end_index": 1052,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This configuration only has two unpaired electrons in the $2p$ subshell, which theoretically would only allow carbon to form two bonds"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1139,
                "end_index": 1306,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "**Excitation:** One electron from the $2s$ orbital is promoted to the empty $2p_z$ orbital, giving carbon four unpaired valence electrons ($2s^1 2p_x^1 2p_y^1 2p_z^1$)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1311,
                "end_index": 1455,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "**Hybridization:** The single $2s$ orbital and the three $2p$ orbitals mix (hybridize) to form **four equivalent $\\text{sp}^3$ hybrid orbitals**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1460,
                "end_index": 1643,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "**Overlapping:** Each of these four $\\text{sp}^3$ hybrid orbitals from carbon overlaps with the spherical $1s$ orbital of a hydrogen atom, creating four identical C\u2013H $\\sigma$ bonds"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1658,
                "end_index": 1830,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Molecular Geometry and Shape\nAccording to the **VSEPR** (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion) theory, the four electron pairs in the C\u2013H bonds repel one another equally"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1832,
                "end_index": 1921,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To minimize this repulsion, they push as far apart as possible in three-dimensional space"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1924,
                "end_index": 1952,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Shape:** **Tetrahedral**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1954,
                "end_index": 2017,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Bond Angle:** Exactly **$109.5^\\circ$** (or $109.47^\\circ$)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2019,
                "end_index": 2105,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Symmetry:** It belongs to the **$T_d$** point group (perfect tetrahedral symmetry)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2120,
                "end_index": 2237,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Key Bond Properties\nAll four C\u2013H bonds in methane are completely identical in terms of length, strength, and energy"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2240,
                "end_index": 2367,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Bond Length:** The distance between the carbon nucleus and each hydrogen nucleus is **$109\\text{ pm}$** ($1.09\\text{ \\AA}$)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2369,
                "end_index": 2533,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Bond Energy:** \n  * The **bond dissociation energy** (the energy required to break the very first C\u2013H bond) is **$439\\text{ kJ/mol}$** ($105\\text{ kcal/mol}$)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2852,
                "end_index": 2895,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The overall molecule** is **non-polar**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2897,
                "end_index": 3066,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because of methane's perfect tetrahedral symmetry, the individual dipole moments of the four C\u2013H bonds pull in opposite directions and cancel each other out completely"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3113,
                "end_index": 3302,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Molecular Orbital (MO) Theory\nWhile **Valence Bond Theory** (which uses $\\text{sp}^3$ hybridization) is excellent for visualizing the shape, tetrahedral geometry, and equal bonds of methane"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3113,
                "end_index": 3419,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Molecular Orbital (MO) Theory\nWhile **Valence Bond Theory** (which uses $\\text{sp}^3$ hybridization) is excellent for visualizing the shape, tetrahedral geometry, and equal bonds of methane, **Molecular Orbital (MO) Theory** provides a different (and more physically accurate) look at the electron energies"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3507,
                "end_index": 3592,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Instead, they occupy delocalized molecular orbitals spread across the entire molecule"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3594,
                "end_index": 3860,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* Photoelectron spectroscopy reveals that methane's valence electrons actually reside in two different energy levels: one lower-energy orbital (derived from carbon\u2019s $2s$ orbital) and three higher-energy, degenerate orbitals (derived from carbon\u2019s $2p$ orbitals)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
